articleSummary:
- As of 17 July 2026, two severe tetanus cases in Vietnam illustrate risk from minor wounds. A 94-year-old man from Hung Yen developed generalized tetanus after a chicken pecked the left heel; family cleaned with water only, no vaccination. Within days he had whole-body stiffness, neck rigidity, trismus, and drooling, requiring intubation before transfer to the National Institute of Infectious and Tropical Diseases. A 50-year-old man from Bac Ninh sustained a left-hand wound (3 x 3 cm, deep to bone) from a fall; initially misdiagnosed; after ICU admission he showed generalized stiffness and recurrent spasms with respiratory failure, diagnosed as severe tetanus. Clostridium tetani is common in soil, dust, animal feces; entry occurs through dirty, open wounds, including small wounds like poult
- On 2026-07-17, in Hung Yen, Vietnam, a 94-year-old man with severe tetanus was admitted to the National Tropical Diseases Hospital after a chicken pecked his left heel ten days earlier. He and his family did not seek medical care or tetanus vaccination. He developed generalized muscle rigidity, severe neck stiffness, trismus, dysphagia, airway obstruction, and required intubation before transfer. Admission labs showed CRP 47 mg/L and leukocytosis (WBC 12.8 x10^9/L). The dry wound served as the entry point for Clostridium tetani. A second recent case involved a 50-year-old man from Bac Ninh with a hand wound who also developed generalized tetanus. Clostridium tetani is common in soil, dirt, animal waste; any open wound can lead to infection if not properly cleaned and vaccinated. Prevention
- Two recent tetanus cases in Vietnam illustrate severe disease after minor wounds. A 94-year-old man from Hung Yen developed generalized tetanus about 10 days after a chicken bite to the left heel; he arrived with respiratory failure, rigidity, and trismus, and required endotracheal intubation in ICU, facing high mortality risk. A second case, a 50-year-old man from Bac Ninh, sustained a hand wound after a fall; initial misdiagnosis delayed recognition, but he later developed generalized tetanus with respiratory failure, necessitating tracheostomy and intensive care with enteral feeding. Clostridium tetani is widespread in soil, dust, manure, and animal/poultry waste and can enter through any dirty wound, especially deep or contaminated ones. Wound cleaning with water alone is insufficient;
